      "text": "% @ Copyright of KAIA, written by Sunghun Kang\n%\n% \\LaTeX\\ Author Guidelines for KAIA - Korean Artificial Intelligence Association\n%\n% Version: 1.0 [2020.07.09]\n\n\\documentclass[nouppercase]{kaia}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\title{Human vs LLM Text Classification Using a Multi-Feature Approach}\n\n% --- 저자 정보 소속 문제 수정 ---\n\\author{Hyungbin Park}{a}\n\\author{Kihoon Lee}{b}\n\\author{Hanwool Kim}{a}\n\\author{Cheoljun Kim}{a}\n\\author{Shinsung Kang}{a}\n\\author{Gwangsu Kim\\textsuperscript{1,}}{b}\n\\affiliation{Department of Statistics, College of Natural Sciences, Jeonbuk National University}{a}\n\\affiliation{Research Institute for Materials and Energy Sciences, Jeonbuk National University}{b}\n\n\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n% --- 1. 제목 페이지 생성 ---\n\\maketitle\n\n% --- 2. 다음 페이지(요약문 시작 페이지)로 이동 (불필요한 \\newpage 제거) ---\n\\newpage\n\n% --- 요약문 시작 (2페이지에 걸쳐 작성) ---\n\\onecolumn\n\t\\begin{center}\n\t\t\\section*{Summary of this paper}\t\n\t\t\\setcounter{subsection}{0}\n\t\\end{center}\n\n% --- 요약문 1페이지 ---\n\\noindent\\fbox{\n    \\parbox{\\textwidth}{\n    \t\\begin{quote}\n            \t\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        Advanced Large Language Models (LLMs) now produce text that is difficult to distinguish from human writing. This creates practical problems in areas requiring source verification, such as ensuring academic integrity and preventing the spread of AI-generated spam. The core challenge is that existing detection methods, which often rely on a single type of feature like word frequency, can be unreliable against modern LLMs.\n\n        Our research addresses this challenge by developing a more robust classification method. We hypothesize that a more accurate distinction is possible by combining multiple types of linguistic features simultaneously. Instead of relying on one signal, we analyze: \n        \\begin{enumerate}\n            \\item \\textbf{Stylistic and Readability Features:} Metrics that describe \\textit{how} the text is written (e.g., sentence complexity, use of personal pronouns).\n            \\item \\textbf{Thematic Features:} Topic distributions derived from Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) that describe \\textit{what} the text is about.\n        \\end{enumerate}\n        \n        This leads to our primary research questions:\n        \\begin{itemize}\n            \\item[\\textbf{Q1:}] To what extent can the fundamental differences between human and LLM texts be quantified through a measurable set of linguistic features?\n            \\item[\\textbf{Q2:}] What is the optimal combination of stylistic and thematic features required to construct a robust computational model for text origin?\n            \\item[\\textbf{Q3:}] Can the proposed multi-feature representation effectively capture the model-specific traces that distinguish one LLM from another?\n        \\end{itemize}\n        \\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\n\t\t\\subsection{Novelty}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        The primary novelty of our work is the The novelty of this work is defined by three key elements that, when combined, create a comprehensive framework for text classification:\n        \n        \\begin{itemize}\n            \\item \\textbf{Hybrid Feature Vector Construction:} Our main contribution is the engineering of a feature set that explicitly combines different sources of information. We concatenate vectors from: (1) \\textbf{Stylistic and Readability analysis} (`textstat`, PRP ratio) and (2) \\textbf{Thematic analysis} (Hierarchical LDA). This creates a single vector that represents both the style and substance of a text, providing a richer basis for classification.\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Hierarchical LDA for Granular Topic Features:} We employ a Hierarchical Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) to model topics. This method allows us to capture not just broad themes but also more nuanced \\textbf{subtopic distributions}. Representing each document with this fine-grained semantic vector provides a more detailed and discriminative feature than standard topic modeling.\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Deep Neural Network for Feature Interaction:} We utilize a Deep Neural Network (DNN) to process the combined feature vector. The rationale for using a DNN is its ability to automatically learn \\textbf{complex, non-linear interactions} between the different feature types (e.g., how a specific topic might correlate with sentence complexity). This allows the model to leverage the full potential of our fused feature set.\n        \\end{itemize}\n        \\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\t\t\\end{quote}\n    }\n}\n\n\n% --- 다음 페이지로 나눕니다 ---\n\\newpage\n\n% --- 요약문 2페이지 ---\n\\noindent\\fbox{\n    \\parbox{\\textwidth}{\n    \t\\begin{quote}      \n\t\t\\subsection{Algorithms}\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        Our methodology is implemented as a systematic four-step pipeline, designed to transform raw text into a rich feature set for robust classification.\n        \n        \\begin{enumerate}\n            \\item \\textbf{Data Collection and Preprocessing:}\n            We began by assembling a balanced corpus comprising texts written by human users (sourced from Quora) and texts generated by four distinct LLMs (Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, and GPT). Each document in the corpus then underwent a standardized preprocessing pipeline using the NLTK library. This included converting text to lowercase, removing special characters and stopwords, and tokenizing the text to prepare it for feature extraction.\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Multi-Feature Engineering:}\n            This is the core of our approach, where we extract a diverse set of quantitative metrics from each preprocessed document. These features fall into two primary categories:\n            \\begin{itemize}\n                \\item \\textbf{Stylistic and Readability Features:} We computed a vector of metrics capturing the structural and stylistic properties of the text. This was achieved using the `textstat` library to calculate standard readability scores (e.g., Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level) and other syntactic stats. Additionally, we calculated the proportion of personal pronouns (PRP) to quantify the degree of personal narrative.\n                \\item \\textbf{Thematic Structure Features:} To capture the substance of the text, we employed a Hierarchical Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) model. This model transforms each document into a numerical vector representing its probability distribution across a set of fine-grained subtopics.\n            \\end{itemize}\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Feature Concatenation:}\n            For each document, the feature vectors generated in the previous step (i.e., the stylistic/readability vector and the thematic vector) were horizontally concatenated. This process creates a single, high-dimensional feature vector that holistically represents the document\u0027s style and substance.\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Classification with a Deep Neural Network (DNN):}\n            The final, fused feature vectors were used as input to train a Deep Neural Network (DNN) classifier. The network architecture was designed with multiple hidden layers and non-linear activation functions (e.g., ReLU). This design enables the model to automatically learn complex, non-linear relationships and interactions within the fused feature space, ultimately predicting the text\u0027s source (Human, GPT, etc.) for a given text.\n        \\end{enumerate}\n\t\t\\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\n        \\subsection{Experiments}\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        To validate the effectiveness of our proposed framework, we conducted a comprehensive experiment designed to test the capability of our fused-feature DNN model.\n\n        \\begin{itemize}\n            \\item \\textbf{Experimental Setup:} The curated dataset, comprising responses from four LLMs and human sources, was partitioned into a training set (80\\%) and a testing set (20\\%). Our Deep Neural Network (DNN) model was trained on the training data using the comprehensive fused feature vectors (stylistic, readability, and thematic) and their corresponding source labels.\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Evaluation and Results:} The model\u0027s performance was rigorously evaluated on the unseen test set. Our DNN classifier achieved an \\textbf{overall accuracy of 73.6\\%}. The performance across each class, measured by the F1-Score, revealed distinct patterns. The model classified human-written text with the highest accuracy, achieving an \\textbf{F1-Score of 0.805}. Among the LLMs, Gemini (F1: 0.777) and GPT (F1: 0.795) were highly recognizable, while the model showed some confusion between Claude and DeepSeek, indicating stylistic similarities between them. A detailed confusion matrix confirmed these per-class performance characteristics.\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Validation Method:} To ensure the robustness and generalizability of these results, a 10-fold cross-validation strategy was employed, which yielded consistent performance metrics across all folds, confirming that the results were not due to a coincidental data split.\n\n            \\item \\textbf{Analysis of Results:} The high F1-score for human-source text empirically confirms that human linguistic patterns are uniquely distinct and measurable. Furthermore, the model\u0027s ability to differentiate between LLMs, even with some confusion, validates our hypothesis that each model possesses a unique, quantifiable \"fingerprint.\" The overall accuracy of 73.6\\% demonstrates that our fused-feature approach provides a powerful and effective basis for text source attribution, even when relying on subtle, non-lexical cues.\n        \\end{itemize}\n\t\t\\end{quote}\n    }\n}\n\n% --- 마지막 요약 페이지의 페이지 번호가 보이지 않게 처리 ---\n\\thispagestyle{empty}\n\n\\end{document}",
