      "text": "% @ Copyright of KAIA, written by Sunghun Kang\n%\n% \\LaTeX\\ Author Guidelines for KAIA - Korean Artificial Intelligence Association\n%\n% Version: 1.0 [2020.07.09]\n\n\\documentclass[nouppercase]{kaia}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\title{Human vs LLM Text Classification Using a Multi-Feature Approach}\n\n% --- 저자 정보 소속 문제 수정 ---\n\\author{Hyungbin Park}{a}\n\\author{Kihoon Lee}{b}\n\\author{Hanwool Kim}{a}\n\\author{Cheoljun Kim}{a}\n\\author{Shinsung Kang}{a}\n\\author{Gwangsu Kim\\textsuperscript{1,}}{b}\n\\affiliation{Department of Statistics, College of Natural Sciences, Jeonbuk National University}{a}\n\\affiliation{Research Institute for Materials and Energy Sciences, Jeonbuk National University}{b}\n\n\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n% --- 1. 제목 페이지 생성 ---\n\\maketitle\n\n \\newpage \n\\newpage\n\n% --- 요약문 시작 (2페이지에 걸쳐 작성) ---\n\\onecolumn\n\t\\begin{center}\n\t\t\\section*{Summary of this paper}\t\n\t\t\\setcounter{subsection}{0}\n\t\\end{center}\n\n% --- 요약문 1페이지 ---\n\\noindent\\fbox{\n    \\parbox{\\textwidth}{\n    \t\\begin{quote}\n            \t\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        Advanced Large Language Models (LLMs) now produce text that is difficult to distinguish from human writing. This creates practical problems in areas requiring source verification, such as ensuring academic integrity and preventing the spread of AI-generated spam. \n\n        Our research develops a classification method utilizing various linguistic features such as lexical density, Personal Pronoun Proportion (PRP), and thematic features. We expect that a more accurate distinction is possible by combining multiple types of linguistic features simultaneously. Instead of relying on one signal, we use: \n        \\begin{enumerate}\n            \\item \\textbf{Stylistic and Readability Features:} Metrics that describe \\textit{how} the text is written (e.g., sentence complexity, use of personal pronouns).\n            \\item \\textbf{Thematic Features:} Topic distributions derived from Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) that describe \\textit{what} the text is about.\n        \\end{enumerate}\n        \n        This leads to our primary research questions:\n        \\begin{itemize}\n            \\item[\\textbf{Q1:}] To what extent can the fundamental differences between human and LLM texts be quantified through a measurable set of linguistic features?\n            \\item[\\textbf{Q2:}] Can the proposed multi-feature representation effectively  distinguish one LLM from others?\n        \\end{itemize}\n        \\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\n\\subsection{Novelty}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        The novelty of this work has two keys contributions that, when combined, create our analytical framework:\n        \n        \\begin{itemize}\n            % [수정] 사용된 구체적인 기술들을 명시하여 내용 보강\n            \\item \\textbf{A Deliberate Combination of Heterogeneous Features:} \n            Our first contribution is the deliberate \\textbf{combination} of two fundamentally different feature categories. We constructed a hybrid vector that integrates (1) a variety of stylistic metrics calculated using libraries like textstat and custom scripts for metrics such as the Personal Pronoun (PRP) ratio, with (2) thematic distributions derived from a Hierarchical Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) model. This approach of fusing stylistic and thematic data into a single vector creates a richer feature set designed to capture a more holistic view of a text\u0027s origin.\n\n% analytical, \n            % 의 역할을 더 명확하게 설명\n            \\item \\textbf{An Analytical Framework for Result Interpretation:}\n            Our second contribution is the analytical framework itself. This approach moves beyond simply reporting accuracy scores and mandates a deeper analysis to explain why the features have potential to discriminate LLMs and human. By this, we can identify the key linguistic fingerprints of each source. For instance, these analyses reveal   that human sources consistently utilize a higher proportion of personal pronouns (PRP), reflecting a distinct narrative style. \n        \\end{itemize}\n        \\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\t\t\\end{quote}\n    }\n}\n% --- 다음 페이지로 나눕니다 ---\n\n\n% --- 요약문 2페이지 ---\n\\noindent\\fbox{\n    \\parbox{\\textwidth}{\n    \t\\begin{quote}      \n\t\t\\subsection{Algorithm}\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        \n        \n        \n        \n        \n        \\begin{enumerate}\n            \\item[] \\textbf{Data Collection and Preprocessing:}\n            The start of our study is carefully constructing corpus. The dataset comprises a total of 14,832 documents, sourced from 5 distinct sources: four LLMs and human sources. For text from human sources, we sourced data from Quora, ensuring all the answers were posted at least five years prior to mitigate potential LLM contamination. We identified approximately 100 unique questions and sampled up to 30 answers per question. To generate the LLM-generated text, we used the same set of questions as prompts for four LLM sources (Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, GPT). The entire corpus then underwent a standardized preprocessing pipeline, including lowercasing, tokenization, and the removal of stopwords and special characters.\n\n\n            \\end{enumerate}\n            \\newpage\n            \\begin{enumerate}\n            Our methodology is implemented as a systematic pipeline, three-step classification process.\\\\\n\n             \n\n              \\textbf{Analytical Pipeline:}\n            The preprocessed data was passed through the following three-step pipeline:\n                    \\begin{center}\n            \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n\n            \\includegraphics[width\u003d1\\linewidth]{boxes.png} \n            \\end{center}\n        \\end{enumerate}\n\t\t\\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\n% box 1.34 키우기 \n\n        \\subsection{Experiments}\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        \n\n        To validate our framework, we conducted a classification experiment using a hold-out validation method. The dataset of 14,832 documents was partitioned into training (80\\%) and test (20\\%) sets with a fixed random state to ensure reproducibility. The DNN model, trained on the comprehensive hybrid features, was rigorously evaluated on the unseen test set and achieved an overall accuracy of 82.2\\%. The detailed results are presented in the figures below.\n        As shown in the confusion matrix(Figure 1) , the model demonstrated high classifying performance, notably classifying human-sourced text with the highest accuracy (F1-Score: 0.902) and differentiating among LLMs with high accuracy. The boxplot of the Personal Pronoun (PRP) ratio (Figure 2) provides human texts exhibit a consistently higher PRP ratio. which serves a powerful classification.\n        Further more, a separate feature impotance analysis confirmed the effectiveness of utilizing various features. While a model trained only on thematic (LDA) features achieved a notable accuracy of 63.2\\%, the full combination of feature yielded a sinigicantly higher performance. This demonstrates that including various features is helpful for capturing the more nuanced differences between sources and maximizing the model\u0027s overall efficacy\n        .\n\n        \\begin{center}\n\n            \\includegraphics[width\u003d0.8\\linewidth]{combined_exp.png}\n        \\end{center}\n\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        Our study successfully demonstrates a framework for classifying texts generated purely by either humans or LLMs. We acknowledge, however, that a significant limitation is its focus on these distinct categories. Real-world scenarios increasingly involve \\textbf{mixed-authorship texts (mixtexts)}, where human writing is revised by AI or vice versa. As highlighted by recent research (e.g., Zhang et al., 2024), detecting such blended texts poses a significant challenge for current methods.\n\n        Therefore, a key direction for our future work is to extend our hybrid feature framework to address the mixtext detection problem. This will involve constructing a new, more complex dataset that includes AI-revised human text and human-revised AI text. Future investigation will then focus on identifying which of our linguistic features remain robust in these nuanced scenarios and exploring new features specifically designed for mixed-text detection.\n\n        \n\t\\end{quote}\n    }\n}\n% --- 마지막 요약 페이지의 페이지 번호가 보이지 않게 처리 ---\n\\thispagestyle{empty}\n\n\\end{document}\n\n\nC. Algorithm\nData Collection and Preprocessing: The start of our study is carefully constructing corpus. The dataset\ncomprises a total of 14,832 documents, sourced from 5 distinct sources: four LLMs and human sources.\nFor text from human sources, we sourced data from Quora, ensuring all the answers were posted at least\nfive years prior to mitigate potential LLM contamination. We identified approximately 100 unique ques-\ntions and sampled up to 30 answers per question. To generate the LLM-generated text, we used the same\nset of questions as prompts for four LLM sources (Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, GPT). The entire cor-\npus then underwent a standardized preprocessing pipeline, including lowercasing, tokenization, and the\nremoval of stopwords and special characters 여기 내용 윗페이지로 다 올려 \n",
