tion method utilizing various linguistic features such as lexical density, Personal Pronoun Proportion (PRP), and thematic features. We expect that a more accurate distinction is possible by combining multiple types of linguistic features simultaneously. Instead of relying on one signal, we use: \n        \\begin{enumerate}\n            \\item \\textbf{Stylistic and Readability Features:} Metrics that describe \\textit{how} the text is written (e.g., sentence complexity, use of personal pronouns).\n            \\item \\textbf{Thematic Features:} Topic distributions derived from Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) that describe \\textit{what} the text is about.\n        \\end{enumerate}\n        \n        This leads to our primary research questions:\n        \\begin{itemize}\n            \\item[\\textbf{Q1:}] To what extent can the fundamental differences between human and LLM texts be quantified through a measurable set of linguistic features?\n            \\item[\\textbf{Q2:}] Can the proposed multi-feature representation effectively  distinguish one LLM from others?\n        \\end{itemize}\n        \\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\n\\subsection{Novelty}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        The novelty of this work has two keys contributions that, when combined, create our analytical framework:\n        \n        \\begin{itemize}\n            % [수정] 사용된 구체적인 기술들을 명시하여 내용 보강\n            \\item \\textbf{A Deliberate Combination of Heterogeneous Features:} \n            Our first contribution is the deliberate \\textbf{combination} of two fundamentally different feature categories. We constructed a hybrid vector that integrates (1) a variety of stylistic metrics calculated using libraries like textstat and custom scripts for metrics such as the Personal Pronoun (PRP) ratio, with (2) thematic distributions derived from a Hierarchical Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) model. This approach of fusing stylistic and thematic data into a single vector creates a richer 