ate our framework, we conducted a classification experiment using a hold-out validation method. The dataset of 14,832 documents was partitioned into training (80\\%) and test (20\\%) sets with a fixed random state to ensure reproducibility. The DNN model, trained on the comprehensive hybrid features, was rigorously evaluated on the unseen test set and achieved an overall accuracy of 82.2\\%. The detailed results are presented in the figures below.\n        As shown in the confusion matrix(Figure 1) , the model demonstrated high classifying performance, notably classifying human-sourced text with the highest accuracy (F1-Score: 0.902) and differentiating among LLMs with high accuracy. The boxplot of the Personal Pronoun (PRP) ratio (Figure 2) provides human texts exhibit a consistently higher PRP ratio. which serves a powerful classification.\n        Further more, a separate feature impotance analysis confirmed the effectiveness of utilizing various features. While a model trained only on thematic (LDA) features achieved a notable accuracy of 63.2\\%, the full combination of feature yielded a sinigicantly higher performance. This demonstrates that including various features is helpful for capturing the more nuanced differences between sources and maximizing the model\u0027s overall efficacy\n        .\n\n        \\begin{center}\n\n            \\includegraphics[width\u003d0.8\\linewidth]{combined_exp.png}\n        \\end{center}\n\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        Our study successfully demonstrates a framework for classifying texts generated purely by either humans or LLMs. We acknowledge, however, that a significant limitation is its focus on these distinct categories. Real-world scenarios increasingly involve \\textbf{mixed-authorship texts (mixtexts)}, where human writing is revised by AI or vice versa. As highlighted by recent research (e.g., Zhang et al., 2024), detecting such blended texts poses a significant challenge for current methods.\n\n        Th