elineskip}\n        Advanced Large Language Models (LLMs) now produce text that is difficult to distinguish from human writing. This presents practical challenges in areas requiring source verification, such as ensuring academic integrity. Our research develops a classification method by integrating multiple linguistic features. We expect that this combined approach yields a more accurate distinction than methods relying on a single signal. We analyze: \n        \\begin{enumerate}\n            \\item \\textbf{Stylistic and Readability Features:} Metrics describing \\textit{how} a text is written.\n            \\item \\textbf{Thematic Features:} Topic distributions (from LDA) describing \\textit{what} a text is about.\n        \\end{enumerate}\n        This leads to our primary research questions: (1) To what extent can the differences between human and LLM texts be quantified? and (2) Can our representation effectively distinguish among different LLMs?\n        \\vspace{1.0\\baselineskip}\n\n        % \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d NOVELTY \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n        \\subsection{Novelty}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        The novelty of this work is defined by two key contributions. First, a \\textbf{deliberate combination of heterogeneous features}, integrating stylistic metrics (e.g., PRP ratio from custom scripts and `textstat` outputs) and thematic distributions (from a Hierarchical LDA model) into a single, rich vector. Second, an \\textbf{analytical framework for result interpretation}. This moves beyond simply reporting accuracy to explain \\textit{why} the model succeeds, by identifying key linguistic fingerprints (e.g., our analysis reveals that human sources consistently utilize a higher PRP ratio).\n        \\vspace{1.0\\baselineskip}\n        \n        %