ic integrity. Our research develops a classification method by integrating multiple types of linguistic features. Instead of relying on a single signal, we analyze both (1) \\textbf{Stylistic and Readability Features}, which describe \\textit{how} a text is written, and (2) \\textbf{Thematic Features} (derived from LDA), which describe \\textit{what} it is about. This leads to our primary research questions: (1) To what extent can the differences between human and LLM texts be quantified? and (2) Can our representation effectively distinguish among different LLMs?\n        \\vspace{1.0\\baselineskip}\n\n        % \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d NOVELTY \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n        \\subsection{Novelty}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        % [수정] 문장 흐름을 자연스럽게 수정\n        The novelty of this work lies in two key contributions that, when combined, create our analytical framework. First, we propose a \\textbf{deliberate combination of heterogeneous features}, integrating stylistic metrics (e.g., PRP ratio from custom scripts and `textstat` outputs) and thematic distributions (from a Hierarchical LDA model) into a single, rich vector. Second, we present an \\textbf{analytical framework for result interpretation}. This moves beyond simply reporting accuracy to explain \\textit{why} the model succeeds by identifying key linguistic fingerprints, revealing, for instance, that human sources consistently utilize a higher proportion of personal pronouns (PRP).\n        \\vspace{1.0\\baselineskip}\n        \n        % \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d ALGORITHM (DATA PART) \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n        \\subsection{Algorithm}\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        Our methodology is comprised of three main stages: data collection, model selection, and an analytical pipeline.\n        \n        \\textbf{Data Collection and Preprocessing:} The foundation of our study is a corpus of \\textbf{14,832 documents} from 5 sources (four LLMs and humans). To mitigate potential LLM contamination, human-sourced texts were collected from Quora answers posted at least five years prior. For approximately 100 unique questions, we sampled up to 30 answers per source. The corpus then underwent a standardized preprocessing pipeline.\n\t\t\\end{quote}\n    }\n}\n% --- 다음 페이지로 나눕니다 ---\n\\newpage\n% --- 요약문 2페이지 ---\n\\noindent\\fbox{\n    \\parbox{\\textwidth}{\n    \t\\begin{quote}      \n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        \\textbf{Model Architecture:} For model selection, we explored three architectures: a tree-based ensemble (LightGBM), an attention-based network (TabNet), and a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP). The MLP was ultimately selected for its superior performance on our dataset. The chosen network consists of four sequential hidden layers, with Batch Normalization and Dropout applied after each layer for regularization.\n\n        \\textbf{Analytical Pipeline:} The preprocessed data was passed through the following three-step pipeline:\n        \\begin{center}\n        \\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\linewidth]{boxes.png} \n        \\end{center}\n        \\vspace{1.5\\baselineskip}\n\n        % \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d EXPERIMENTS \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n        \\subsection{Experiments}\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        To validate our framework, we conducted a classification experiment using hold-out validation. The dataset was partitioned into training (80\\%) and test (20\\%) sets with a fixed random state for reproducibility. The selected MLP model, trained on the hybrid features, was evaluated on the test set and achieved an \\textbf{overall accuracy of 82.2\\%}. \n        \n        The detailed results are presented in the figures below. As shown in the confusion matrix (Figure 1), the model demonstrated high performance, successfully classifying human-sourced text with the highest accuracy (F1-Score: \\textbf{0.902}) and differentiating among the LLMs. The boxplot of the Personal Pronoun (PRP) ratio (Figure 2) reveals that human texts exhibit a consistently higher PRP ratio, which serves as a powerful feature for classification.\n        \n        % [수정] 오타 수정 및 문장 구조 변경, 쉼표 추가\n        Furthermore, a feature importance analysis confirmed the effectiveness of our hybrid approach. A model trained only on thematic (LDA) features achieved an accuracy of 63.2\\%, whereas combining thematic features with stylistic features, such as PRP and lexical density, increased the accuracy to 78.9\\% and 81.2\\%, respectively. This demonstrates that while thematic features provide a strong baseline, the combination with stylistic features is crucial for capturing nuanced differences and maximizing performance.\n\n        \\begin{center}\n            \\includegraphics[width\u003d0.85\\linewidth]{12345.png}\n        \u003c/end{center}\n\n\t\t\\vspace{0.5\\baselineskip}\n        % [수정] 문장 흐름 및 단어 선택 수정\n        While our framework is effective for distinct sources, future work should extend this approach to detect more complex, mixed-authorship texts (mixtexts). As highlighted by recent research (e.g., Zhang et al., 2024), developing features and models robust to this growing challenge remains a key area for investigation.\n\t\\end{quote}\n    }\n}\n% --- 마지막 요약 페이지의 페이지 번호가 보이지 않게 처리 ---\n\\thispagestyle{empty}\n\n\\end{document}\n```\n\n### **교열된 내용 상세 설명**\n\n#### **1페이지**\n\n1.  **제목:**\n    *   `Hu